Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:01.640
OK.

00:00:01.640 --> 00:00:05.220
I have a gas slight alert for you today.

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:10.660
Gaslighting is when you manipulate someone
into questioning their own ability to understand

00:00:10.660 --> 00:00:11.660
the world.

00:00:11.660 --> 00:00:17.080
And the new gaslight phrase of the week from
the Trump administration is we are now testing

00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:19.640
more than South Korea per capita.

00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:25.740
And this is this is going to be a painful
one because it is such a weaponized this information.

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:28.109
But I do think it's important to do this.

00:00:28.109 --> 00:00:33.800
This is the anatomy of disinformation for
you to understand very, very clearly.

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:37.020
So we have a corona virus pandemic.

00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:41.149
The way we find cases is by doing tests for
the virus.

00:00:41.149 --> 00:00:44.680
Right now, they they've all been RNA based
tests.

00:00:44.680 --> 00:00:48.240
An antigen test is now in development and
may soon be available.

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:53.879
But so far, you find cases by doing RNA tests,
which require a number of different elements

00:00:53.879 --> 00:00:55.109
in order to do the test.

00:00:55.109 --> 00:00:56.280
So far, so good.

00:00:56.280 --> 00:01:00.890
South Korea and the United States find their
first respective cases of Corona virus within

00:01:00.890 --> 00:01:01.890
hours of each other.

00:01:01.890 --> 00:01:03.260
We often say January 20th.

00:01:03.260 --> 00:01:05.110
I think one was January 19.

00:01:05.110 --> 00:01:06.320
The other was the 20th.

00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:08.940
With the time difference, it may have been
simultanous.

00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:09.940
Doesn't matter.

00:01:09.940 --> 00:01:15.430
Within hours of each other, South Korea and
the United States confirmed their first case.

00:01:15.430 --> 00:01:21.600
South Korea immediately developed a test,
ramps up testing capacity tests.

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:27.240
Lots of people establish his fever clinics
to isolate people who test positive contact

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:33.040
traces, exposed contacts of infected people,
puts them in home isolation, and they do a

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:37.280
fantastic job with the virus, all while not
being authoritarian.

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:42.520
Remember, South Korea is ranked higher than
the United States on the Demark Democracy

00:01:42.520 --> 00:01:43.520
Index.

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:44.520
That was January.

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:46.090
Now, Trump didn't do that.

00:01:46.090 --> 00:01:49.579
We didn't get much testing capacity until
the end of March, which is.

00:01:49.579 --> 00:01:55.570
Essentially, two whole months during which
South Korea developed tested contact rates,

00:01:55.570 --> 00:01:58.320
etc., we started to ramp up testing at the
end of March.

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:02.630
If you looked at the numbers of tests that
we were doing in the first part of March,

00:02:02.630 --> 00:02:05.759
it was numbers like 600 tests in an entire
day.

00:02:05.759 --> 00:02:08.340
We got up to a few thousand tests per day.

00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:10.929
Our testing capacity was terrible at the time.

00:02:10.929 --> 00:02:15.349
We were about 50th or 50 fifth in the world
per capita, maybe even worse.

00:02:15.349 --> 00:02:16.790
I don't remember exactly.

00:02:16.790 --> 00:02:22.069
Now compare our testing numbers in early March
to South Korea's positive test results by

00:02:22.069 --> 00:02:23.230
early March.

00:02:23.230 --> 00:02:28.840
South Korea was finding sometimes eight hundred
positive cases in a day.

00:02:28.840 --> 00:02:33.810
And there was a period in early March where
South Korea was doing so much testing that

00:02:33.810 --> 00:02:39.080
they were finding more cases than the total
number of tests we were doing.

00:02:39.080 --> 00:02:40.080
Understand that comparison.

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:41.709
There were days in early March.

00:02:41.709 --> 00:02:46.440
We South Korea and the U.S. had our first
confirmed case on the same day.

00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:52.480
Days in March where we would do X number of
tests and South Korea would have that same

00:02:52.480 --> 00:02:57.510
X number of positive tests because they were
doing so much more testing than us.

00:02:57.510 --> 00:03:00.600
This allowed South Korea to stop the spread
very, very early.

00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:08.400
Now, over time, during March, April into May,
our testing rate continued to really lag.

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:14.430
We were testing 50th in the world than forty
fifth in the world per capita than forty first.

00:03:14.430 --> 00:03:18.780
Currently, our testing rate is about thirty
thousand people per million.

00:03:18.780 --> 00:03:22.370
That puts us 40th worldwide in testing per
capita.

00:03:22.370 --> 00:03:24.140
This is as of a few hours ago.

00:03:24.140 --> 00:03:29.000
We are behind Singapore and Canada in per
capita testing, but we are ahead of the Czech

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:31.090
Republic as examples.

00:03:31.090 --> 00:03:37.170
And as we were failing during March and April,
reporters kept asking Trump and other officials.

00:03:37.170 --> 00:03:41.120
You keep saying we're doing a lot of tests,
but what about per capita?

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:42.609
Because per capita we're not.

00:03:42.609 --> 00:03:47.190
It wasn't clear whether Trump understood what
per capita meant and or was pretending not

00:03:47.190 --> 00:03:48.190
to.

00:03:48.190 --> 00:03:50.120
We didn't know the talking point was.

00:03:50.120 --> 00:03:56.200
We've done more tests than anyone in the aggregate,
but of course, per capita was what was relevant

00:03:56.200 --> 00:03:57.280
at the time.

00:03:57.280 --> 00:04:02.020
The new talking point now is, hey, everybody's
talking about South Korea.

00:04:02.020 --> 00:04:06.350
We now have more per capita tests than South
Korea.

00:04:06.350 --> 00:04:07.350
That is true.

00:04:07.350 --> 00:04:12.489
And in fact, our testing per capita is now
double that of South Korea's.

00:04:12.489 --> 00:04:14.209
But why is this gaslighting?

00:04:14.209 --> 00:04:18.400
It's gaslighting because South Korea barely
has any cases.

00:04:18.400 --> 00:04:24.800
There is no point now in comparing us to South
Korea because South Korea barely has an outbreak

00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:26.390
at this point in time.

00:04:26.390 --> 00:04:28.870
Look again at South Korea's curve.

00:04:28.870 --> 00:04:34.970
South Korea hasn't had more than 150 cases
in a day since March 11th.

00:04:34.970 --> 00:04:35.970
That's not a typo.

00:04:35.970 --> 00:04:41.990
I want to say that again, South Korea has
not had more than 150 cases since March 11th

00:04:41.990 --> 00:04:42.990
in the US.

00:04:42.990 --> 00:04:48.290
We regularly have a thousand fifteen hundred
two thousand twenty five hundred deaths per

00:04:48.290 --> 00:04:49.290
day.

00:04:49.290 --> 00:04:54.890
South Korea has not had a day with more than
150 cases since March Eleven.

00:04:54.890 --> 00:04:56.230
That's two months ago.

00:04:56.230 --> 00:05:02.310
So the point is, of course, our per capita
testing rate is now greater than South Korea's.

00:05:02.310 --> 00:05:05.760
They have nobody to test because almost no
one has the virus.

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:07.900
We have a third of all the global cases.

00:05:07.900 --> 00:05:11.560
South Korea has 258 deaths.

00:05:11.560 --> 00:05:13.850
Total, total.

00:05:13.850 --> 00:05:15.910
South Korea has two hundred and fifty eight
deaths.

00:05:15.910 --> 00:05:21.220
The Trump administration is now bragging that
our per capita testing rate is higher than

00:05:21.220 --> 00:05:24.450
South Korea's, but it's actually an embarrassment.

00:05:24.450 --> 00:05:30.850
It took months for us to get our testing rate
to match that of a country that essentially

00:05:30.850 --> 00:05:33.880
eliminated the outbreak two months ago.

00:05:33.880 --> 00:05:37.620
And we are still only 40th in testing per
capita.

00:05:37.620 --> 00:05:44.670
There is no serious reason to even mention
South Korea anymore other than to say that's

00:05:44.670 --> 00:05:46.170
what we should have done.

00:05:46.170 --> 00:05:48.560
They handled it correctly and we didn't.

00:05:48.560 --> 00:05:50.730
This is the new Trump is talking point.

00:05:50.730 --> 00:05:52.090
Trump is using it.

00:05:52.090 --> 00:05:55.060
Kaili McEnany used it yesterday during her
press briefing.

00:05:55.060 --> 00:05:57.220
We need to be testing more.

00:05:57.220 --> 00:06:00.810
South Korea doesn't even have sick people
to test anymore.

00:06:00.810 --> 00:06:05.670
There's no reason to continue comparing ourselves
to South Korea at this point.

00:06:05.670 --> 00:06:10.950
South Korea is an instructive example for
what we should have done on January 20.

00:06:10.950 --> 00:06:12.480
That's almost three months ago.

00:06:12.480 --> 00:06:13.690
Let's take a quick break.

